{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by the political instability of the Crusader states. He inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Due to his youth the government was managed by regents until he came of age. His mother Hodierna played a key role in his upbringing and early rule. Raymond III was raised in the court of Jerusalem and was closely tied to the royal family. He was a cousin of King Baldwin III and later King Amalric I. His connection to the royal house gave him influence but also made him a target in court politics.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a central figure in the internal conflicts of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was often at odds with other nobles and kings over policy and power. He opposed the aggressive military strategies of King Amalric I and later King Baldwin IV. Raymond believed in diplomacy and alliances with Muslim powers rather than constant warfare. He formed a secret alliance with Nur ad-Din and later with Saladin. This caused suspicion among his Christian peers who viewed him as a traitor. His reputation suffered greatly as a result. He was briefly imprisoned by Baldwin IV after being accused of treason. The charges were never proven but the damage to his standing was lasting. Despite this he remained a powerful noble with control over Tripoli and its resources.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nIn his later years Raymond III was named regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the illness of Baldwin IV. He held this position from 1183 to 1185. His regency was marked by political tension and military weakness. He failed to prevent the rise of Guy of Lusignan who was married to Baldwin IVs sister Sibylla. Raymond opposed Guy and was eventually removed from the regency. He spent his final years in Tripoli preparing for the inevitable Muslim advance. He died in 1187 shortly after the Battle of Hattin. His death came before the fall of Jerusalem and he was not present at the final collapse of the kingdom. Raymond III is remembered as a cautious and intelligent noble who struggled to balance survival with loyalty in a fractured world. His legacy is one of quiet diplomacy overshadowed by the chaos of his time.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.007803904823958874}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.11431466042995453}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.2874520507466514e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.000753476400859654}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00015352977789007127}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.8742269680369645e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.3132808208465576}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0016019619069993496}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.3841830625315197e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.645074735279195e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2.622600959512056e-06}, {"token": "1"